            0.33,
            0.026,
            0.838,
            0.044
          ],
          "content": "Chapter 8 — Nature of Matter: Elements, Compounds, and Mixtures"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.888,
            0.029,
            0.922,
            0.045
          ],
          "content": "131"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.083,
            0.429,
            0.105
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Keep the curiosity alive"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.123,
            0.743,
            0.162
          ],
          "content": "1. Consider the following reaction where two substances, A and B, combine to form a product C:"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.373,
            0.164,
            0.487,
            0.181
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "A + B C"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.185,
            0.744,
            0.242
          ],
          "content": "Assume that A and B cannot be broken down into simpler substances by chemical reactions. Based on this information, which of the following statements is correct?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.16,
            0.243,
            0.743,
            0.28
          ],
          "content": "(i) A, B, and C are all compounds and only C has a fixed composition."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.279,
            0.741,
            0.299
          ],
          "content": "(ii) C is a compound, and A and B have a fixed composition."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.299,
            0.74,
            0.317
          ],
          "content": "(iii) A and B are compounds, and C has a fixed composition."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.317,
            0.742,
            0.353
          ],
          "content": "(iv) A and B are elements, C is a compound, and has a fixed composition."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.356,
            0.422,
            0.374
          ],
          "content": "2. Assertion: Air is a mixture."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.377,
            0.743,
            0.415
          ],
          "content": "Reason: A mixture is formed when two or more substances are mixed, without undergoing any chemical change."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.161,
            0.416,
            0.744,
            0.454
          ],
          "content": "(i) Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.155,
            0.455,
            0.743,
            0.493
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Both Assertion and Reason are true, but Reason is not the correct explanation for Assertion."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.494,
            0.557,
            0.514
          ],
          "content": "(iii) Assertion is true, but Reason is false."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.515,
            0.558,
            0.535
          ],
          "content": "(iv) Assertion is false, but Reason is true."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.537,
            0.744,
            0.593
          ],
          "content": "3. Water, a compound, has different properties compared to those of the elements oxygen and hydrogen from which it is formed. Justify this statement."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.127,
            0.594,
            0.743,
            0.632
          ],
          "content": "4. In which of the following cases are all the examples correctly matched? Give reasons in support of your answers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.633,
            0.568,
            0.652
          ],
          "content": "(i) Elements — water, nitrogen, iron, air."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.155,
            0.652,
            0.709,
            0.671
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Uniform mixtures— minerals, seawater, bronze, air."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.671,
            0.732,
            0.689
          ],
          "content": "(iii) Pure substances— carbon dioxide, iron, oxygen, sugar."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.689,
            0.743,
            0.708
          ],
          "content": "(iv) Non-uniform mixtures — air, sand, brass, muddy water."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.707,
            0.744,
            0.783
          ],
          "content": "5. Iron reacts with moist air to form iron oxide, and magnesium burns in oxygen to form magnesium oxide. Classify all the substances involved in the above reactions as elements, compounds or mixtures, with justification."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.823,
            0.534,
            0.868
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Prepare some questions based on your learnings so far ..."
        },
